# Model compression comparison bench

Compare **knowledge distillation**, **quantization**, and **pruning** on the same small CNN and dataset so results are comparable.

The default task is **CIFAR-10**, a standard 10-class image-classification benchmark: 50,000 training images and 10,000 test images, each 32 × 32 × 3. torchvision downloads it into `data/` (not committed) when you run the experiment.

Default YAML epochs (3 / 3 / 1) are a short local check, not a published accuracy number. Bump `epochs_teacher` / `epochs_student` / `epochs_finetune` before treating the table as a real result.

## One command

From the repo root (Windows, macOS, or Linux):

```text
python scripts/setup.py
python scripts/test.py
python scripts/run.py
```

`setup.py` creates `.venv` and installs the package (CPU PyTorch if there is no NVIDIA GPU). `test.py` runs unit tests with no dataset download. `run.py` runs the Fashion-MNIST comparison into `results/fashion_mnist`.

Static INT8 uses whichever quantized engine this PyTorch build provides (`x86`, `fbgemm`, `onednn`, or `qnnpack`). CUDA is used when `torch.cuda.is_available()`.

```text
python scripts/run.py --dataset cifar10
python scripts/run.py --curve --epochs 30
```

## Pick epoch count (Option A)

The YAML epoch values are a smoke test. To pick a real `epochs_teacher` (and matching `epochs_student`), train **only the teacher**, log **held-out validation accuracy** after each epoch, and stop looking when the curve flattens. The official test set is not used for this choice.

```text
python scripts/run.py --curve --epochs 30
```

On a typical laptop CPU that is about **1–2 hours** for 30 Fashion-MNIST epochs. The script writes `results/fashion_mnist/teacher_curve.csv` and `teacher_curve_best.pt`, and prints `Suggested epochs_teacher`. Copy that number into `epochs_teacher` and `epochs_student` in the YAML, then run `python scripts/run.py` once. If it prints `none`, raise `--epochs` and rerun (or inspect the CSV).

`run_comparison.py` still trains on the full training set and scores on the test set.

## What is compared

| Variant | Technique | What it measures |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `teacher_fp32` | baseline | Accuracy / size / latency of the larger fp32 CNN |
| `student_ce` | smaller model, hard labels only | Capacity vs distillation |
| `student_kd` | distillation (KL + CE) | Whether soft teacher labels beat CE for the same student |
| `teacher_pruned` | global unstructured L1 prune (+ optional fine-tune) | Sparsity vs accuracy |
| `teacher_dynamic_int8` | dynamic INT8 on Linear layers | Easy quantization path |
| `teacher_static_int8` | post-training static quant (calibrate + convert) | Stronger INT8 path; skipped automatically if the backend fails |

Shared metrics: top-1 accuracy, parameter count, nonzero params, sparsity, serialized `state_dict` size (MB), batch latency p50/p95.

## Setup

```text
python scripts/setup.py
```

## Sample grid

```text
python scripts/save_sample_grid.py --data-dir data --out results/cifar10_samples.png
```

## Other entry points

```text
python scripts/run_comparison.py --config configs/default.yaml --out results/cifar10
python scripts/run_comparison.py --skip-static-quant
```

## Tests (no dataset download)

```text
python scripts/test.py
```

## Layout

- `src/compression_bench/models.py` — teacher/student CNNs with quant stubs
- `src/compression_bench/distill.py` — temperature-scaled KD loss
- `src/compression_bench/prune.py` — global unstructured magnitude prune
- `src/compression_bench/quantize.py` — dynamic + static PTQ (auto engine)
- `src/compression_bench/evaluate.py` — accuracy, size, latency
- `scripts/setup.py` — create `.venv` and install deps
- `scripts/test.py` — unit tests (no dataset)
- `scripts/run.py` — portable entry (Fashion-MNIST comparison or `--curve`)
- `scripts/run_comparison.py` — end-to-end sweep → `results/comparison.csv`
- `scripts/train_teacher_curve.py` — teacher val-accuracy curve → `results/.../teacher_curve.csv`
- `scripts/save_sample_grid.py` — labeled CIFAR-10 class grid → `results/cifar10_samples.png`
- `configs/default.yaml` — CIFAR-10 default
- `configs/fashion_mnist.yaml` — smaller CPU alternative

## Notes

- Unstructured pruning zeros weights but **does not shrink serialized size** unless you use a sparse format or structured prune. The `sparsity` and `nonzero_params` columns are the honest signal.
- Dynamic quant mainly shrinks **Linear** layers; conv compute stays fp32.
- Distillation quality depends on a decent teacher. Bump `epochs_teacher` before judging KD.
- Static INT8 needs a quantized engine compiled into PyTorch. `backend: auto` picks one that exists on this install.
